  "notes": "`pop-2020s` did not claim her (the volume says it checked), so she is entried here on the R&B-and-Latin-percussion material. The modelled band is the trap-soul one — \"120–140 read / 60–70 felt half-time for the trap-soul cuts\" — with the dembow/Latin kit carried as the percussion layer rather than as a second model, because it is what the entry treats as her signature: \"timbales/congas as the main fill instrument\". So the perc block is the widest and loudest in this batch, and `kick.tresilloBias` is high for the 3+3+2 figure the entry names. The harmony follows the entry's two shapes — minor 2-chord loops with m9 pads, and Latin/reggaeton-adjacent modal vamps written out as `i–VI–VII` and `i–V`. The melody is \"agile mid-soprano with high rhythmic density, staccato syllabic phrasing designed for choreography\", so `articulation` is staccato, the density is high and the runs are short — `endVariation` on, `phraseBars` short. ⚠ `confidence` is **low**: the research rates the entry medium-low and says \"tempo bands and drum detail are idiom inference\". ⚠ Every drum number, the swing amount, the 808 figures and both melody bands are therefore **INFERRED**; the researched facts are the era, the two tempo bands, the backbeat classes, the two harmonic shapes and the named percussion. ⚠ `session.bpm.mode` is **INFERRED**: the number is not stated in the entry, and it is an authored centre of gravity inside the band the entry does state. ⚠ **`session.keys` is INFERRED.** The entry's Keys line describes the harmonic grammar but names no key centre, so these values and their weights are an authored choice — no key in this set is stated in the research.",
